\value{
A list of \code{\link[ggplot2:ggplot]{ggplot2::ggplot()}} objects.
}
\description{
Plots smooth and parametric contributions from a fitted GAM model using
\code{\link[ggplot2:ggplot]{ggplot2::ggplot()}}.
For models fitted by \code{\link[mgcv:gamm]{mgcv::gamm()}}, \code{assemble()} and \code{\link[=draw]{draw()}} use the
fitted \code{gam} component.
}
\details{
Smooth plot subtitles identify the model part or distributional
parameter as described in \code{\link[=draw.gam]{draw.gam()}}. Custom smooth labels, including
\code{subtitle}, can be passed through \code{...}. Use \code{cyclic = "polar"} to draw
one-dimensional cyclic smooths with polar coordinates; \code{polar_args}
customizes the coordinate display as described in \code{\link[=draw.gam]{draw.gam()}}.
}
\note{
Internally, plots of each smooth are created using \code{\link[ggplot2:ggplot]{ggplot2::ggplot()}}
and composed into a single plot using \code{\link[patchwork:wrap_plots]{patchwork::wrap_plots()}}. As a
result, it is not possible to use \code{+} to add to the plots in the way one
might typically work with \code{ggplot()} plots. Instead, use the \code{&} operator;
see the examples.
}
\examples{
load_mgcv()

# simulate some data
df1 <- data_sim("eg1", n = 400, dist = "normal", scale = 2, seed = 2)
# fit GAM
m1 <- gam(y ~ s(x0) + s(x1) + s(x2) + s(x3), data = df1, method = "REML")

# assemble plots of all smooths
assemble(m1)

# can be passed to patchwork::wrap_plots()
suppressPackageStartupMessages(library("patchwork"))
assemble(m1) |> wrap_plots(ncol = 2, nrow = 2)

# Prepare individual plots from a GAMM
m2 <- gamm(y ~ s(x0) + x1, data = df1, method = "REML")
gamm_plots <- assemble(m2)
gamm_plots[[1]]
}
